  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nTONIGHT AND FRIDAY:\n\nCool advection and cloud cover have kept temperatures on the \nchilly side today, with low 50s only across portions of \nnorthwest WI that cleared out earlier this morning. Breezy \nnorthwest winds will diminish this evening, as high pressure \nslides into the region from the west. Expect cloud cover to \ncontinue eroding through the afternoon and evening as drier air \narrives. With winds becoming light, temperatures will fall into \nthe 20s overnight. Would not be surprised to see a few places in\nthe upper teens in north central MN. \n\nCool to neutral cold advection in low levels will continue \nthrough part of the day Friday, while low level winds remain\nunder the influence of the surface high. This will keep\ntemperatures on the cool side, with highs in the mid 40s to mid\n50s.\n\nWEEKEND: \n\nWarm advection develops Friday night as winds turn to the south\nand high pressure slides to the east. A warm front will lift\nnorthward, bringing scattered showers to the Northland\nSaturday. Although, coverage could be as much as 40-50 percent\naround mid day, little to no accumulation is expected. There\ncould still be lingering showers around Saturday evening.\nHighs Saturday will be in the 50s, with temperatures falling \ninto the 40s after sunset. \n\nWith continued southerly flow, temperatures continue to warm \nSunday, with highs 10 to 20 degrees above normal. Afternoon \nhighs will be in the 60s for most of the area, with mid to upper\n50s along the Shores of Lake Superior. Overnight lows remain \nwell above freezing.\n\nEARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nBy Monday and Tuesday, the chance for widespread precipitation \nreturns as a larger and better organized storm system moves \ninto the central CONUS. Model guidance is in better agreement\nwith a surface low developing lee of the Rockies Monday and\nmoving toward the Upper Midwest as a longwave trough moves\nthrough the Central CONUS. This will bring rainfall back to the\nNorthland Monday. A second system, a clipper moving\nsoutheastward through central Canada, will arrive Tuesday,\nmaintaining rain chances for the area. How these two systems\ninteract and how quickly they move to the east will dictate how\nlong rain chances hang around the Northland. Right now, it looks\nlike we could be in a wetter pattern through at least \nWednesday. This will bring widespread beneficial rainfall to the\narea, with temperatures remaining warmer than normal.",
